      ],
      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**Negotiate. I would not accept this offer as-is if compensation is a major priority and you\u2019re happy at Meta.**\n\nThe offer is not terrible, but it does not currently provide a compelling financial reason to leave:\n\n- **Google four-year annualized TC:** approximately **$458,000**\n  - $215,000 base\n  - $43,000 target bonus\n  - $200,000 annualized new-hire equity\n- **Your current annualized Meta TC:** approximately **$526,000**\n- **Difference:** Google is about **$68,000/year lower**, or roughly **13% below** your current package.\n\nAt the offered cash compensation, Google would need to raise the equity grant from **$800,000 to approximately $1.07 million** merely to match your current $526,000 annualized compensation\u2014not to pay you a premium for changing jobs.\n\nCurrent reported market data also suggests this is toward the lower end for a Bay Area Google L6 PM. Levels.fyi currently reports approximately **$606,000 median TC for Bay Area L6 PMs**, while a broader recent dataset puts L6 median base around **$240,000** and median total compensation around **$514,000**. These figures mix existing employees and new offers, so they are directional rather than definitive. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\n## The vesting schedule is crucial\n\nBefore evaluating the offer conclusively, get the exact vesting schedule in writing.\n\nGoogle new-hire grants are often reported as front-loaded\u2014commonly **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**, though other schedules are also reported. Do not assume yours follows that pattern. ([career.cornell.edu](https://career.cornell.edu/blog/2025/04/22/understanding-your-job-offer-its-not-just-about-the-salary/?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf your $800,000 follows 38/32/20/10, target compensation would look approximately like this, ignoring stock-price changes and refreshers:\n\n| Year | Base + bonus | Initial RSUs | Total |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | $258K | $304K | **$562K** |\n| 2 | $258K | $256K | **$514K** |\n| 3 | $258K | $160K | **$418K** |\n| 4 | $258K | $80K | **$338K** |\n\nThat changes the interpretation:\n\n- Year 1 would beat your stated **next-12-month Meta compensation of about $476,000**.\n- Year 2 would be close to your normal Meta TC.\n- Years 3 and 4 would fall dramatically unless refresh grants make up the difference.\n\nGoogle does have an annual compensation process that includes modeled equity refresh recommendations based on factors such as performance, location, and role, with manager discretion. But that does not turn future refreshes into guaranteed compensation. ([services.google.com](https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\n**Value refreshers at zero when deciding whether to accept.** Treat them as upside until granted.\n\n## What I would ask for\n\nI would make one clear, package-level counter:\n\n- **Base:** $255,000\n- **New-hire RSUs:** $1.2 million\n- **Sign-on bonus:** $150,000\n\nTell them you have flexibility in how they allocate the value, but that you need a materially stronger overall package.\n\nThat request produces approximately:\n\n- $255K base\n- $51K target bonus\n- $300K annualized initial equity\n- **$606K normalized annual TC**\n- Plus the one-time sign-on\n\nThat is an assertive counter, but it is supportable because:\n\n1. Your current annualized compensation is $526K.\n2. You are not seeking to leave Meta.\n3. Your current base is already $25K above their offer.\n4. Their $215K base is only around 36% of the way through the posted $183K\u2013$271K range.\n5. Current reported Bay Area L6 PM compensation supports a normalized package in this neighborhood. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n6. Sign-on and equity are commonly negotiable components; equity is often the highest-leverage portion of a PM offer. ([igotanoffer.com](https://igotanoffer.com/en/advice/product-manager-salary-negotiation?utm_source=openai))\n\n### A realistic settlement target\n\nI would feel reasonably good about:\n\n- **Base:** $245K\u2013$255K\n- **RSUs:** $1.05M\u2013$1.2M\n- **Sign-on:** $75K\u2013$150K\n\nFor example:\n\n- $250K base\n- $50K target bonus\n- $1.1M equity\n- $100K sign-on\n\nThat is **$575K normalized annual compensation**, plus the sign-on. It provides a modest but real premium over Meta.\n\nIf Google has limited flexibility, prioritize components in this order:\n\n1. **RSUs**\n2. **Base**\n3. **Sign-on**\n\nEquity most directly fixes the multiyear compensation gap. Base is recurring and affects the target bonus. Sign-on helps with transition costs and forfeited Meta equity, but does not fix years 2\u20134.\n\n## Your suggested script\n\nYou can say:\n\n> I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and role. At the same time, I\u2019m happy at Meta and wasn\u2019t actively looking, so the financial case needs to be compelling for me to make the move.\n>\n> My current annual compensation is approximately $526,000, including $240,000 base, approximately $36,000 bonus, and about $250,000 in annual equity. I also have approximately $200,000 vesting over the next 12 months.\n>\n> The current Google package annualizes to approximately $458,000 before any future refresh grants, which is meaningfully below my current compensation, and the base is also $25,000 below my current base.\n>\n> A package that would make the move compelling would be approximately $255,000 base, $1.2 million in new-hire equity, and a $150,000 sign-on bonus. I\u2019m flexible about the allocation among components if there are constraints, but I would need the overall guaranteed package to be materially closer to that level.\n>\n> Could you take that back to the compensation team?\n\nThen stop talking. Don\u2019t volunteer your minimum.\n\nIf the recruiter says they need a competing offer, respond:\n\n> I don\u2019t have another external offer because I wasn\u2019t running a broad search. My alternative is remaining at Meta at approximately $526,000 in annual compensation, which is a very real and attractive option for me.\n\nYour current compensation and willingness to stay are legitimate leverage.\n\n## Questions to get answered before accepting\n\nAsk the recruiter to confirm in writing:\n\n1. **What percentage of the $800K vests in each of the four years?**\n2. **When is the first vest, and are subsequent vests monthly or quarterly?**\n3. **Will you be eligible for the next annual refresh cycle, or is there a tenure cutoff?**\n4. **How do refresh grants vest?**\n5. **Can they provide a typical\u2014not promised\u2014refresh range for an L6 PM receiving a standard performance rating?**\n6. **Is the bonus prorated in the first year?**\n7. **What is the sign-on repayment period if they add one?**\n8. **Is the grant stated as a fixed dollar value converted at grant, or a specified number of shares?**\n\n## When I would accept versus walk\n\n### I would accept if:\n\n- The role is at least as attractive as Meta from a scope and career perspective, **and**\n- They reach approximately **$575K in normalized annualized compensation**, with some sign-on protection.\n\nA package around **$250K base + $1.1M RSUs + $100K sign-on** would clear that bar for me.\n\n### I might accept at a lower level if:\n\n- The Google role has unusually strong leadership, scope, promotion potential, or strategic value to your career.\n- You specifically want to diversify away from Meta.\n- The exact vesting schedule creates a strong first-two-year outcome and you are comfortable reassessing before the later equity cliff.\n\n### I would probably walk if:\n\n- They stay near **$215K base and $800K equity** with no sign-on.\n- They justify the gap primarily through non-guaranteed refreshers.\n- The job itself is merely comparable to your current role rather than clearly better.\n\nYou are not choosing between unemployment and Google. You are choosing between a job you already like at roughly **$526K** and a riskier transition. Google should pay at least a modest switching premium\u2014not ask you to take a persistent discount and hope that future refreshes repair it.",
